WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.840
Let's talk about various ways to use Git.

00:02.840 --> 00:07.520
We can use Git on the command line, so we open a terminal or command prompt window to

00:07.520 --> 00:09.360
execute Git commands.

00:09.360 --> 00:13.720
This is the fastest and sometimes the easiest way to get the job done.

00:13.720 --> 00:15.760
That's why a lot of people use the command line.

00:15.760 --> 00:20.280
Now if you don't like the command line, well, you're in luck, because most modern code

00:20.280 --> 00:24.520
editors and IDEs have built-in support for basic Git features.

00:24.520 --> 00:28.680
For example, in VS Code, we have this source control panel which gives us the essential

00:28.680 --> 00:29.920
Git features.

00:29.920 --> 00:33.920
There are also extensions available for bringing additional Git features.

00:33.920 --> 00:37.320
For VS Code, the most popular extension is Git Lens.

00:37.320 --> 00:40.080
It brings a ton of Git features in VS Code.

00:40.080 --> 00:44.520
There are also graphical user interfaces specifically made for using Git.

00:44.520 --> 00:47.800
Here on the Git website, you can find the complete list of these tools for different

00:47.800 --> 00:48.800
platforms.

00:48.800 --> 00:53.560
We have tools for Windows, Mac, Linux, Android, and iOS.

00:53.560 --> 00:58.640
Out of all these, the two most popular tools are Git Kraken and Source Tree.

00:58.640 --> 01:01.840
Git Kraken is my personal favorite GUI tool for Git.

01:01.840 --> 01:06.040
It's beautifully designed, it works across different platforms, and it integrates with

01:06.040 --> 01:11.080
other Git Kraken products such as Git Kraken boards for issue tracking, and Git Kraken

01:11.080 --> 01:13.480
timelines for project management.

01:13.480 --> 01:18.000
It's free for open source projects, but for commercial projects, you have to pay an annual

01:18.000 --> 01:19.000
fee.

01:19.000 --> 01:22.360
Just to let you know, I'm not an affiliate of Git Kraken, I'm just a fan.

01:22.360 --> 01:24.200
The other option is Source Tree.

01:24.200 --> 01:28.000
It's completely free, but it's only available for Windows and Mac.

01:28.000 --> 01:32.360
So if you're a Linux user, you have to use Git Kraken or another GUI tool.

01:32.360 --> 01:36.040
In this course, we'll be spending most of our time on the command line for a couple

01:36.040 --> 01:37.120
of reasons.

01:37.120 --> 01:41.000
The first reason is that pretty much all of these GUI tools have some limitations.

01:41.000 --> 01:44.280
They support the Git commands that people use most of the time.

01:44.280 --> 01:48.360
So even if you want to use a GUI tool, there are times you would have to roll up your sleeves

01:48.360 --> 01:51.360
and get back to the command line to get the job done.

01:51.360 --> 01:55.200
The other reason is that the command line is always available, but there are situations

01:55.200 --> 01:58.200
where a GUI tool may not be available to you.

01:58.200 --> 02:02.160
You might connect to a server remotely, and you may not have permission to install a GUI

02:02.160 --> 02:03.160
tool.

02:03.160 --> 02:06.160
If you don't know how to use the command line, then you're stuck.

02:06.160 --> 02:11.000
In practice, a lot of people, including myself, use both the command line and a GUI tool,

02:11.000 --> 02:13.000
and that's what I'm going to show you in this course.

02:13.000 --> 02:16.560
There are times that it really makes sense to use a GUI tool and not the command line,

02:16.560 --> 02:20.560
but there are other times that using the command line is faster and easier, so we'll use the

02:20.560 --> 02:21.720
command line.

02:21.720 --> 02:24.480
At the end of the day, you should use the right tool for the job.

02:24.760 --> 02:25.960
Don't be like this guy.

02:25.960 --> 02:27.200
You probably know him.

02:27.200 --> 02:30.560
Our popular superstar developer called John Smith.

02:30.560 --> 02:34.280
He thinks he's better than everyone else because he does everything in the command

02:34.280 --> 02:35.280
line.

02:35.280 --> 02:37.960
He never uses a GUI tool and he looks down at people who do.

02:37.960 --> 02:41.480
Well, let him think whatever he wants if that's what makes him happy.

02:41.480 --> 02:42.480
Who cares?

02:42.480 --> 02:45.960
So, we'll be spending most of our time on the command line, but when it makes sense

02:45.960 --> 02:50.640
to use a GUI tool, I'll be showing you examples in VS Code and Git Kraken because those are

02:50.640 --> 02:52.160
the most popular tools.

02:52.160 --> 02:55.120
Now, if you have never worked with a command line before, don't worry.

02:55.120 --> 02:58.640
I'm going to hold your hands and teach you everything step by step.

02:58.640 --> 03:00.400
It's a lot easier than you think.

03:00.400 --> 03:01.680
So next, we're going to install Git.

